WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.420
Last episode, our service was pure business logic.

2
00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.820
But the auth, logging and tenancy have to live somewhere.

3
00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:13.100
That somewhere is a service policy.

4
00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:16.470
A service policy is an implementation of a

5
00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:19.890
horizontal requirement that cuts across services.

6
00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:22.450
In Chenile, a policy is an interceptor

7
00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:25.100
that runs around a service operation.

8
00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:29.010
A request comes in and becomes a ChenileExchange.

9
00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:31.550
It passes through the interceptors

10
00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:33.990
— security, tenancy, i18n, logging

11
00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:38.830
— reaches the implementation, and travels back out the same way.

12
00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.820
Every interceptor follows three rules.

13
00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:42.190
One:

14
00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:43.340
one and only one.

15
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:46.310
Each interceptor implements a single policy,

16
00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:48.470
and only one interceptor owns it

17
00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:51.490
— so there's exactly one place to fix it.

18
00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:51.860
Two:

19
00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:53.310
configuration-driven.

20
00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:57.500
It's steered by typed extension keys, supplied at deploy time

21
00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:58.920
— not hard-coded.

22
00:00:59.020 --> 00:00:59.410
Three:

23
00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:00.830
orchestration-unaware.

24
00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:02.760
It never assumes its position.

25
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.210
It may say before or after the payload

26
00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:07.780
— but it never hard-wires the order.

27
00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:11.650
Those rules make a policy testable in isolation,

28
00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:15.530
reusable across services, and changeable in one place.

29
00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:19.590
The exact opposite of the duplicated plumbing from episode two.

30
00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:20.940
Here's one:

31
00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.840
a request-logging interceptor.

32
00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:26.510
It extends BaseChenileInterceptor, and does one thing.

33
00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.320
Attach it by configuration

34
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.610
— in the service registry, or with one annotation.

35
00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:34.630
The service implementation doesn't change.

36
00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:36.890
Same behavior, two ways.

37
00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:39.500
One is tangled into the business logic.

38
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:43.310
The other is a clean implementation plus one interceptor.

39
00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:45.640
Only one of them you can reuse.

40
00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:48.760
So if a policy isn't welded to a service

41
00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:50.120
— where should it run?

42
00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:50.430
Next:

43
00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:52.600
the gateway, and the last mile.
